# Punar default application handlers — installed as /etc/xdg/mimeapps.list.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. Without it nothing in Punar answered `xdg-open
# https://...`, which is how one application asks the system to open a link:
# the terminal's URL activation, a notification action, a chat client, the
# command center's "open" verb. Chromium shipped in the image and was bound to
# PUNAR+B, so a human could reach a browser — but the SYSTEM could not, and
# every path that was not the keybind failed silently.
#
# RESOLUTION ORDER (XDG mime-apps spec, and the reason this path is the right
# one): handlers are read from $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/mimeapps.list first, then from
# each $XDG_CONFIG_DIRS entry, which defaults to /etc/xdg. A user who sets
# their own default browser therefore WINS over this file rather than fighting
# it: Punar states a default, the user states a preference, and the preference
# is not something Punar has to be asked for permission to override.
#
# The browser id below is Punar's generic product entry. Its Exec target is the
# closed browser argv builder, so links, the key binding, the command center,
# and curated web fallbacks all select the same browser context and security
# defaults. A missing desktop id here fails OPEN, so the live image check
# asserts both resolution and file presence rather than trusting this text.

[Default Applications]
x-scheme-handler/http=punar-browser.desktop
x-scheme-handler/https=punar-browser.desktop
text/html=punar-browser.desktop
application/xhtml+xml=punar-browser.desktop
inode/directory=thunar.desktop
